WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.680
Now let's start with performance&nbsp;
metrics for a classification problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.080
In this lesson, we focus on describing metrics&nbsp;
for evaluating binary classification problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.920
for example predicting whether patients&nbsp;
would develop heart failure or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.400
These metrics can be generalized to the setting&nbsp;
with multiple classes as well. But in this lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.080
we'll focus on the binary setting. There are&nbsp;
many evaluation metrics for classification model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.760
Next, we'll illustrate how they're all&nbsp;
connected. To depending on the prediction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:40.320
and the Ground Truth value, we have four different&nbsp;
possible outcomes. True Positive, False Positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:46.480
False Negative and True Negative. Next, we explain&nbsp;
their definition and relationship to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.000
So, the output of a binary predictive&nbsp;
model, can be either Positive or Negative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.960
Here, you can see a zoom in version of this. To&nbsp;
differentiate with the Ground Truth value, we call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.800
this Prediction Outcome Positive, and Prediction&nbsp;
Outcome Negative. Similarly, we have the Ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.040
Truth value can be either Positive and Negative.&nbsp;
Again, to differentiate with the prediction value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.800
we call this Condition Positive and Condition&nbsp;
Negative. If the Prediction Outcome is positive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.720
and the Ground Truth's value is also positive,&nbsp;
then we call this, True Positive. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.880
if the Prediction Outcome is positive, but&nbsp;
the Ground Truth condition is negative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.360
then we call this False Positive, or Type I error.&nbsp;
Conversely, if the Prediction Outcome is negative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.240
and the Ground Truth Condition is positive,&nbsp;
we call this False Negative, or Type II error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.320
Finally, a True Negative occur, when Prediction&nbsp;
Outcome and Ground Truth Condition are both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.880
Negative. In fact, this 2x2 matrix is called&nbsp;
a Confusion Matrix, or a Contingency table.&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.120
&nbsp;
Now, we understand all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.560
the definitions of this basic matrix. Let's&nbsp;
look at the relationship among this matrix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:05.520
First, each row and column of this matrix sum&nbsp;
up to the marginal. For example, True Positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.440
plus False Positive equals Prediction Outcome&nbsp;
Positive. And the True Positive plus the False&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.440
Negative equals the Condition Positive. And the&nbsp;
Total Population equals the Prediction Outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:25.280
Positive plus Prediction Outcome Negative. Or&nbsp;
Condition Positive plus Condition Negative.

